{"model": "gemini-3.8-flash", "qid": "q005", "dataset": "sat_math", "para": 3, "sample": "g", "temp": 0.0, "answer": "A", "correct": true, "content_len": 539, "reasoning_len": 0, "snippet": "We are given the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& y < -x + a \\\\\n& y > x + b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd told that $(0,0)$ is a solution. So substitute $x = 0$, $y = 0$ into both inequalities:\n\n1. $0 < -0 + a \\Rightarrow a > 0$\n2. $0 > 0 + b \\Rightarrow b < 0$\n\nSo we know $a > 0$ and $b < 0$. Therefore, $a > b$ must be true.\n\nThe other options:\n- (B) $b > a$: false, since $b < 0 < a$\n- (C) $|a| > |b|$: not necessarily — e.g., $a = 1, b = -5$: both satisfy, but $|a| < |b|$\n- (D) $a = -b$: not required\n\nSo the correct answer is (A).\n\nAnswer: (A)", "usage": {"pt": 418, "ct": 229}, "t": 1792301916.9}
{"model": "gemini-3.8-flash", "qid": "q005", "dataset": "sat_math", "para": 4, "sample": "g", "temp": 0.0, "answer": "A", "correct": true, "content_len": 101, "reasoning_len": 0, "snippet": "If $(0,0)$ is a solution, then $0 < a$ and $0 > b$, so $a > 0 > b$, which means $a > b$.\n\nAnswer: (A)", "usage": {"pt": 421, "ct": 45}, "t": 1792301967.6}
